es","libraries offer audio","libraries offer audiobooks","libraries include audio","library book formats","audio in library","library reading formats","library accessible formats","talking book library","library format classification","audio book lending","audiobook lending","library recognition","institutional reading practice","library reading service","books in different formats"],"tier":"familiar","points":25,"reveal":"Libraries offer books in audio and braille as accessible reading formats. Institutional reading practice can be broader than print.","counters":[]},{"id":"for_playback_controls","side":"for","answer":"Playback controls","aliases":["pause","rewind","pause and rewind","playback speed","speed control","change speed","playback control","audio controls","skip back","go back","chapter navigation","browse chapters","repeat a passage","replay","listen again","adjust speed","variable speed","audio pacing","you can pause","you can rewind","control the pace","control playback speed","adjust narration speed","change narration speed","rewind a passage","replay a passage","relisten to passage","jump to chapter","navigate by chapter","skip backward","skip forward","stop and replay","pause to think","repeat audio passage"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"You can pause, rewind, change speed, and navigate chapters. Audio need not be a conveyor belt you cannot control.","counters":[]},{"id":"for_bookmarks","side":"for","answer":"Audio notes","aliases":["bookmarks","bookmark","audio bookmark","audio bookmarks","audio clips","clips and notes","listening notes","audio annotation","audio annotations","bookmark passages","save a quote","save clips","take notes","annotate audio","annotating audio","clip a passage","audio can be annotated","audio supports notes","audio supports bookmarks","bookmarks in audio","notes in audio","annotate while listening","make audio notes","keep listening notes","save audio passage","mark audio passage","mark a passage","bookmark a quotation","clip a quote","notes and clips","audio study tools","active audio study"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"Audio can have bookmarks, clips, and notes too. Active revisiting is not reserved for a paper margin.","counters":[]},{"id":"for_active_attention","side":"for","answer":"Focused listening","aliases":["active listening","attentive listening","paying attention","pay attention","focused attention","focus","concentrated listening","close listening","listen carefully","careful listening","intentional listening","engaged listening","active engagement","attention choice","listen with focus","undistracted listening","listen attentively","listen with attention","listen without distraction","concentrate on audio","concentrate while listening","pay close attention","deliberate listening","intentional attention","focused audiobook listening","actively follow narration","listen actively","engage with audio","concentrated attention","focus on the words","attend to the book","careful audio attention"],"tier":"familiar","points":25,"reveal":"A listener can choose to pay close attention. Headphones do not automatically make the encounter passive.","counters":[{"targetId":"against_passive_multitasking","reveal":"You can choose focused listening rather than multitasking. Audio is not inherently passive."}]},{"id":"for_braille","side":"for","answer":"Braille comparison","aliases":["braille","tactile reading","reading by touch","touch reading","not just eyes","not just sight","nonvisual reading","non visual reading","reading without sight","reading without eyes","eyes not required","sensory flexibility","different senses","touch and sight","sensory routes","braille is reading","braille reading","blind reading","reading need not sight","reading not only sight","reading not just eyes","read without sight","read without eyes","read through touch","touch based reading","reading uses touch","reading can be tactile","tactile written reading","more than eyesight","beyond sight","senses can differ"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"Braille shows that reading is not limited to seeing ink. The category already accommodates more than one sensory route.","counters":[{"targetId":"against_eye_strain","reveal":"Braille is reading without seeing ink. An eyes-only rule cannot define every ordinary use of reading."}]},{"id":"for_author_narration","side":"for","answer":"Author narration","aliases":["author reads","author reading","read by author","read by the author","author narrated","author narrated memoir","author voice","authors voice","own voice","self narration","self narrated","writer narrates","writer reading","author audiobook","becoming","michelle obama","author as narrator","author is narrator","narrated by author","narrated by the author","read by writer","writer reads aloud","author reads aloud","author performs text","author performs own work","writer narrates own work","author telling own story","author telling memoir","author spoken words","direct author voice","direct authorial delivery","writer voice"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"Sometimes the author is the narrator. The voice can be an authorial delivery rather than a stranger’s replacement.","counters":[]},{"id":"for_translation_analogy","side":"for","answer":"Translation analogy","aliases":["translated books","translation","translations","read in translation","different language","translated novel","translation counts","translation comparison","language changes","different wording","book in translation","translated text","same work translation","translation still reading","translation example","translations still count","translations count as reading","translated work counts","translated book counts","same book different language","same work different language","reading translated books","reading translated novels","reading a translation","translations count","language change analogy","translation remains reading","translation of a book","translation of text","reading in another language","different language same book"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"We count translated books as reading despite a changed verbal route. Exact surface delivery is not always what people mean by reading a book.","counters":[]},{"id":"for_broad_definition","side":"for","answer":"Broad reading sense","aliases":["broad definition","broad sense","broad reading","broad meaning","reading as understanding","read means understand","reading means understanding","interpretation","interpreting meaning","understanding a text","meaning of read","reading definition","wider definition","wider sense","reading broadly","reading as meaning","reading as interpretation","read as understand","read as interpret","broader reading definition","broader meaning of read","wider reading usage","broader reading usage","broad ordinary meaning","multiple senses of read","multiple reading senses","word read is broad","read has many meanings","understand or interpret","interpret the book","meaning focused reading"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"Read can mean understanding or interpreting as well as decoding writing. Ordinary language can support a wider book-reading use.","counters":[{"targetId":"against_different_verb","reveal":"Read has wider uses as well as a narrow activity label. Delivery verbs need not settle a personal book-reading count."}]},{"id":"against_visual_decoding","side":"against","answer":"Written symbol decoding","aliases":["visual text decoding","visual decoding","decoding","written symbols","written text","symbol decoding","text decoding","decode text","decode writing","letters","written letters","printed symbols","print decoding","decoding letters","sight or touch","reading symbols","decoding written words","decoding printed words","processing written symbols","reading written marks","written word decoding","written symbol processing","symbols on page","letters and symbols","recognizing written words","recognising written words","written language processing","written code","decode written language","written text processing","read written symbols","written marks"],"tier":"obvious","points":10,"reveal":"In the narrower literacy sense, reading decodes written symbols through sight or touch. Audio decoding is a different task.","counters":[]},{"id":"against_passive_multitasking","side":"against","answer":"Divided attention","aliases":["passive multitasking","multitasking","doing chores","driving","washing dishes","background audio","background listening","passive listening","distracted listening","divided focus","split attention","attention divided","doing other things","working while listening","half listening","listening while busy","audio while doing chores","audio while driving","listening during chores","listening during driving","listening while cooking","listening while cleaning","doing chores while listening","doing dishes","washing up","distracted by chores","background audiobook","audio as background","passive background audio","attention elsewhere","not fully engaged","multitasking listener"],"tier":"familiar","points":25,"reveal":"Hands-free playback makes multitasking easy. Hearing the words while doing chores does not itself establish engagement with the book.","counters":[]},{"id":"against_narrator_performance","side":"against","answer":"Narrator performance","aliases":["voice actor","narrator","narration","acting","voice acting","performed narration","narrator interpretation","narrator emphasis","emotional delivery","voice choices","performed text","character voices","accents","dramatic interpretation","performance choices","spoken interpretation","narrator adds interpretation","narrator chooses tone","narrator chooses emphasis","voice actor interpretation","someone else chooses voices","different narrative delivery","acted voices","performed character voices","narrator emotion","narrator tone","narrator accents","emotional tone","voice inflection","interpretive voice","external interpretation","performed voices"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"A narrator supplies timing, emphasis, and voices. Those performance choices add an interpretation beyond the words on the page.","counters":[{"targetId":"for_inner_voice","reveal":"Your own inner voice and a narrator’s chosen emphasis are not necessarily the same experience."}]},{"id":"against_different_verb","side":"against","answer":"Listening versus reading","aliases":["listening vs reading","different verb","listen not read","listened not read","listening","hearing","auditory activity","you listened","listening to book","ears versus text","different action","activity words","different verbs","literal activity","different delivery verb","listened rather than read","heard rather than read","heard a book","listened to book","activity is listening","activity was listening","listening not reading","hearing not reading","precise activity label","precise verb","literal verb","delivery route","hearing a recording","listening to recording","auditory delivery"],"tier":"obvious","points":10,"reveal":"For describing the activity precisely, listened and read distinguish the delivery route. Different verbs can be useful.","counters":[]},{"id":"against_skimming_pacing","side":"against","answer":"Parallel scanning","aliases":["pacing control","skimming","skim","scanning","scan the page","visual scanning","scan paragraphs","page overview","paragraph overview","jump between lines","read at a glance","visual navigation","scan back","rereading a line","skim several lines","parallel text view","view several lines","view whole paragraph","see whole paragraph","scan several paragraphs","skip visually","jump visually","move eyes backward","reread visually","skim text visually","look back at paragraph","visual page overview","nonlinear visual access","page at a glance","text at a glance","sequential versus parallel","audio is sequential"],"tier":"familiar","points":25,"reveal":"A page lets you skim several lines and jump visually between passages. Sequential audio does not present that same view, even with controls.","counters":[]},{"id":"against_podcast_analogy","side":"against","answer":"Podcast analogy","aliases":["podcasts and radio","podcast","podcasts","radio","radio story","audio documentary","spoken podcast","history podcast","narrative podcast","listening to radio","radio analogy","podcast comparison","podcast listening","radio documentary","spoken broadcast","audio story","understanding a podcast","understanding radio","podcast is listening","radio is listening","spoken media comparison","spoken media analogy","podcast not reading","radio not reading","audio broadcast","audio narrative comparison","history radio program","narrative radio","radio story comparison","podcast story comparison","spoken content analogy","radio content analogy"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"Understanding a spoken podcast does not usually get called reading it. Spoken comprehension alone may be too broad a definition.","counters":[{"targetId":"for_story_comprehension","reveal":"A podcast can be understood too. Comprehension alone does not distinguish reading a book."}]},{"id":"against_spelling_punctuation","side":"against","answer":"Written spelling","aliases":["spelling and punctuation","spelling","orthography","letter shapes","word spelling","see the spelling","seeing words","written form","homophones","spelled words","word shapes","letters on page","spelling exposure","visual word form","punctuation marks","seeing written words","seeing letter patterns","written word shapes","visual spelling","spelling of words","exact written spelling","orthographic information","orthographic form","orthographic exposure","letter order","written punctuation","visible punctuation","visible word forms","textual spelling","seeing punctuation","seeing orthography"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"You can follow speech without seeing how its words are spelled. That orthographic part of reading is not supplied by sound alone.","counters":[]},{"id":"against_drifting_off","side":"against","answer":"Playback is not attention","aliases":["mind wandering","drifting off","falling asleep","sleeping","asleep","not paying attention","progress bar","playback completion","empty room","book plays itself","unattended playback","finished playback","auto play","unheard chapters","missed chapters","attention not completion","audio keeps playing","audio plays unattended","file finishes unattended","playback can finish","finish without listening","finish without attention","completed while asleep","asleep while playing","sleep through chapters","miss the narration","words go unheard","progress without understanding","completion without attention","no attentive listener","nobody in room","unattended audiobook"],"tier":"familiar","points":25,"reveal":"Let the audiobook finish in an empty room. A completed progress bar does not mean somebody read or understood the book.","counters":[{"targetId":"for_finishing_the_book","reveal":"The file can finish without an attentive listener. Playback completion alone is not proof of reading."}]},{"id":"against_different_medium","side":"against","answer":"Distinct art form","aliases":["distinct art forms","different medium","audio medium","audio production","separate medium","different format","performance art","spoken art","recorded performance","audio art","medium matters","form matters","performed book","separate art form","medium distinction","format distinction","different artistic medium","separate artistic medium","different artistic form","audio artistic form","own art form","separate production","audio rendition","audio adaptation form","recorded literary performance","audio performance medium","form of delivery","medium is meaningful","artistic medium difference","different literary experience","separate audio form","different aesthetic form"],"tier":"clever","points":50,"reveal":"A performed audio production has its own artistic form. A 